n Dallas for a night and  we went on to Tampa we decided to go to  Tampa because we have another series the  Mets that we're actually gonna play this  weekend but at the time we didn't know  where we were going and what was going  to happen and so we ended up going to  Tampa and so for guys that packed for a  three-day road trip the reality of a you  know 18 game road trip and the fact that  their families were displaced and they  couldn't get ahold of them  it had an emotional toll and I think  it's showed in the way we've played the  last couple days but we got a bit of  good news that the mayor really wanted  us to play here in Houston this weekend  mayor Sylvester Turner his leadership  ceman phenomenal minute maid park was  unscathed we had a little bit of water  damage but it was in the lower bowels of  the stadium and nothing anywhere that  could keep us from playing and so  yesterday we announced that we're going  to play the Mets this weekend a  doubleheader on Saturday and one game on  Sunday here at Minute Maid and it has  brought a ray of sunshine to the city  had tremendous outpouring of support  people excited that we're going to be  playing because folks want a distraction  and they want to get back to normal and  I think we're gonna help do that on  Saturday we're talking with Reed Ryan  he's the team president of the Houston  Astros they obviously have played  recently down in Tampa against the  Rangers because they've been displaced  by Hurricane Harvey you know I guess my  question is Reed you know I'm an NFL guy  and I saw that the Cowboys and the  Texans canceled that game so that in  part the Texans players could get back  to Houston and help their families help  everything going on there in the city  was that a conversation I know that's a  preseason game but was that a  conversation at all at the major league  baseball level about you know letting  the guys come back so they can make sure  that their their families and everything  was safe we